+## LLDB/Homebrew installer boundary
+
+The agents-infra bootstrap has one optional mutation before either managed
+binary is replaced: `scripts/setup.sh:93-170,258-263` normally runs
+`install_lldb_mcp`, which can run `brew install llvm` and rewrite
+`$(brew --prefix)/bin/lldb-mcp`. On the measured target, Homebrew and the
+wrapper are present at `/opt/homebrew/bin/lldb-mcp` (SHA-256
+`1ea8dd5ab2f944cca608d6facd14e590769879c821cfa9a4f85f7e89a9b86d2d`), so the
+mutating branch is reachable; absence is not assumed. Homebrew reports
+`llvm 23.1.0` installed with prefix `/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm`, and against that
+successfully read prefix the wrapper's configured
+`/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/bin/lldb-mcp` target and adjacent `lldb` executable are
+measured absent; invoking the wrapper exited 1. The prefix read succeeding is
+what makes those two absences facts rather than artifacts. That is
+a pre-existing baseline anomaly, not a migration result; the lockstep steps
+must preserve the exact presence/absence shape and do not claim to repair LLDB.
+
+Steps 3 and 6 must therefore invoke the reviewed release bootstrap with
+`AGENTS_INFRA_SKIP_LLDB_MCP=1`. The production function returns at lines 94-96
+before `brew --prefix`, `brew install llvm`, wrapper backup/removal, or helper
+resolution. LLDB bootstrap is unrelated to this contract migration and is
+deferred to a separate reviewed maintenance operation.
+
+The following read-only identity guard runs immediately before and after each
+bootstrap. It covers the Homebrew llvm version, wrapper, helper, and adjacent
+LLDB executable; a missing read or byte/metadata difference fails the step.
+
+```bash
+snapshot_lldb_surface() (
+  # Subshell body plus an explicit status test on every read. Neither the
+  # caller's shell options nor an `if` context can turn a failed read into a
+  # zero status, and nothing here depends on `errexit` semantics that differ
+  # between bash and zsh.
+  set -euo pipefail
+
+  command -v brew >/dev/null || exit 70
+  command -v stat >/dev/null || exit 70
+  command -v shasum >/dev/null || exit 70
+  command -v grep >/dev/null || exit 70
+
+  brew_prefix=""
+  if ! brew_prefix="$(brew --prefix)"; then exit 70; fi
+  if test -z "$brew_prefix"; then exit 70; fi
+  printf 'BREW_PREFIX|%s\n' "$brew_prefix"
+
+  # `brew list --versions llvm` exits 1 both when llvm is genuinely absent and
+  # when the query itself fails, so it cannot distinguish a measured absence
+  # from a failed read. The full installed-formula list is unambiguous: exit 0
+  # makes the list authoritative, and any non-zero status refuses.
+  installed_formulae=""
+  if ! installed_formulae="$(brew list --formula)"; then exit 70; fi
+  if printf '%s\n' "$installed_formulae" | grep -qx llvm; then
+    llvm_installed=yes
+  else
+    llvm_installed=no
+  fi
+
+  set -- "$brew_prefix/bin/lldb-mcp"
+  if test "$llvm_installed" = yes; then
+    llvm_versions=""
+    if ! llvm_versions="$(brew list --versions llvm)"; then exit 70; fi
+    if test -z "$llvm_versions"; then exit 70; fi
+    printf 'LLVM_INSTALLED|%s\n' "$llvm_versions"
+
+    llvm_prefix=""
+    if ! llvm_prefix="$(brew --prefix llvm)"; then exit 70; fi
+    if test -z "$llvm_prefix"; then exit 70; fi
+    printf 'LLVM_PREFIX|%s\n' "$llvm_prefix"
+
+    # The llvm-prefixed targets are probed only when the prefix was actually
+    # read. Deriving them from an unset prefix collapses them to `/bin/...`
+    # and reports a failed read as `ABSENT`.
+    set -- "$@" "$llvm_prefix/bin/lldb-mcp" "$llvm_prefix/bin/lldb"
+  else
+    printf 'LLVM_NOT_INSTALLED|\n'
+  fi
+
+  for target in "$@"; do
+    if test -e "$target" || test -L "$target"; then
+      printf 'PRESENT|'
+      if ! stat -f '%N|%HT|%z|%m|%Sp' "$target"; then exit 70; fi
+      if ! shasum -a 256 "$target"; then exit 70; fi
+    else
+      printf 'ABSENT|%s\n' "$target"
+    fi
+  done
+)
+
+capture_lldb_surface() (
+  # Same construction: subshell body, explicit statuses. This function is
+  # invoked as an `if` condition, where a braced body would run with `errexit`
+  # suppressed and a failed `mv` would publish nothing while returning zero.
+  set -euo pipefail
+  destination="$1"
+  partial="${destination}.partial.$$"
+  rm -f "$destination" "$partial"
+
+  snapshot_exit=0
+  snapshot_lldb_surface > "$partial" || snapshot_exit=$?
+  if test "$snapshot_exit" -ne 0; then
+    rm -f "$partial"
+    exit "$snapshot_exit"
+  fi
+  if ! test -s "$partial"; then
+    rm -f "$partial"
+    exit 71
+  fi
+  # Publish only after the snapshot fully succeeded, so partial evidence can
+  # never be compared as if it were complete.
+  if ! mv -f "$partial" "$destination"; then
+    rm -f "$partial"
+    exit 72
+  fi
+  exit 0
+)
+
+guarded_agents_infra_install() (
+  set -euo pipefail
+  release_source="$1"
+  guard_dir="$2"
+  mkdir -p "$guard_dir"
+  rm -f "$guard_dir/passed"
+
+  before_exit=not-run
+  install_exit=not-run
+  after_exit=not-run
+  cmp_exit=not-run
+
+  if capture_lldb_surface "$guard_dir/before.txt"; then
+    before_exit=0
+    if AGENTS_INFRA_SKIP_LLDB_MCP=1 "$release_source/scripts/setup.sh"; then
+      install_exit=0
+    else
+      install_exit=$?
+    fi
+    if capture_lldb_surface "$guard_dir/after.txt"; then
+      after_exit=0
+      if cmp -s "$guard_dir/before.txt" "$guard_dir/after.txt"; then
+        cmp_exit=0
+      else
+        cmp_exit=$?
+      fi
+    else
+      after_exit=$?
+    fi
+  else
+    before_exit=$?
+  fi
+
+  printf 'before_exit=%s\ninstall_exit=%s\nafter_exit=%s\ncmp_exit=%s\n' \
+    "$before_exit" "$install_exit" "$after_exit" "$cmp_exit" \
+    > "$guard_dir/status.txt"
+
+  if test "$before_exit" = 0 && test "$install_exit" = 0 && \
+    test "$after_exit" = 0 && test "$cmp_exit" = 0; then
+    : > "$guard_dir/passed"
+    exit 0
+  fi
+  exit 1
+)
+```
+
+An explicit `ABSENT` line is a measured baseline fact; failure to execute a
+read is not absence and fails the guard. Three constructions enforce that, and
+each is covered by a negative probe that is red without it:
+
+1. **Every read has an explicit status test.** `snapshot_lldb_surface` and
+   `capture_lldb_surface` have subshell bodies that set their own
+   `set -euo pipefail` and additionally test each command with
+   `if ! var="$(...)"; then exit 70; fi`. A braced function body invoked as an
+   `if` condition runs with `errexit` suppressed, and `var=$(cmd)` does not
+   trip `errexit` in zsh at all — so neither the caller's `if` context nor its
+   shell dialect can turn a failed read into a zero status. The probes are
+   executed under both `bash` and `zsh` for exactly this reason.
+2. **The llvm state is read through an unambiguous query.**
+   `brew list --versions llvm` exits 1 both when llvm is genuinely absent and
+   when the query fails, so it cannot separate a measured absence from a failed
+   read. The guard instead requires `brew list --formula` to exit 0 and treats
+   that list as authoritative; any non-zero status refuses.
+3. **A path is never derived from an unread prefix.** The two llvm-prefixed
+   targets are probed only after `brew --prefix llvm` succeeded. Deriving them
+   from an unset prefix collapses them to `/bin/lldb-mcp` and `/bin/lldb` and
+   emits `ABSENT` lines for paths that were never the intended targets — a
+   failed read wearing the shape of a measured absence.
+
+`capture_lldb_surface` deletes partial
+and stale destination files unless the whole snapshot exits zero, and requires
+a non-empty published snapshot.
+`guarded_agents_infra_install` records the real status of all four operations;
+`not-run` is distinct from zero. It creates `passed` only after the before
+snapshot, installer, after snapshot and `cmp` each exited zero. Identical
+partial bytes therefore cannot authorize the installed surface. The interval
+from the before snapshot through the successful `cmp` is an LLDB bypass guard,
+not an accepted mixed-install window: the required outcome is zero
+LLDB/Homebrew delta. If the `passed` file is absent, report the result as
+`unknown/failed`, withhold all new default-PATH composition, and do not claim
+W3/W6 rollback success. The release operator must restore that separately owned
+Homebrew surface or obtain its owner before the migration resumes; this plan
+neither mutates nor claims to roll it back.
+
+## Recovery snapshot and restore primitives
+
+Run this before Step 2 and repeat into a new `bridge-pair` snapshot after both
+`0.25.0` and `v1.7.0` pass installed canaries. These commands copy no
+credential, cookie, token, keychain data or environment value.
+
+```bash
+set -euo pipefail
+
+extract_reported_commit() {
+  version_text="$1"
+  parsed="$(printf '%s\n' "$version_text" | sed -nE \
+    -e 's/.*\(commit ([0-9a-f]{7,40}),.*/\1/p' \
+    -e 's/.* commit=([0-9a-f]{7,40})( .*)?$/\1/p')"
+  if ! test -n "$parsed"; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  if ! test "$(printf '%s\n' "$parsed" | awk \
+    'NF { count++ } END { print count + 0 }')" -eq 1; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  printf '%s\n' "$parsed"
+}
+
+require_saved_binary_source_identity() {
+  binary_name="$1"
+  source_tree="$2"
+  version_text="$3"
+  if ! source_head="$(git -C "$source_tree" rev-parse HEAD)"; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  if ! reported_commit="$(extract_reported_commit "$version_text")"; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  if ! reported_head="$(git -C "$source_tree" \
+    rev-parse "$reported_commit^{commit}")"; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  if ! test -z "$(git -C "$source_tree" status --porcelain)"; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  if ! test "$reported_head" = "$source_head"; then
+    return 1
+  fi
+  printf '%s source_head=%s reported_commit=%s\n' \
+    "$binary_name" "$source_head" "$reported_commit"
+}
+
